\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate the pipeline on two LIBERO task suites~\cite{liu2023libero}, asking: (i)~does learned correction improve over the frozen VLA? (ii)~what is the relative contribution of selection vs.\ correction? (iii)~how does conditioning interact with magnitude? (iv)~where does the pipeline fail?

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Benchmarks and base VLA.}
We use LIBERO-Object (10 tasks, novel object instances) and LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks, spatial reasoning). Each task is evaluated over 10 rollouts (max 300 steps); success is binary.
We build on SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M parameters, flow matching), using dual-camera RGB ($256{\times}256$) and 8D proprioceptive state. Actions are 7D (6D pose + 1D gripper); the gripper is thresholded outside the pipeline.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
All use the same frozen SmolVLA: \textbf{VLA-only} ($K{=}1$, no correction), \textbf{Random} ($K{=}5{/}10$, uniform sample, no CRM), and \textbf{Argmin} ($K{=}5{/}10$, $L_2$ selection, no CRM).

\paragraph{Training and statistics.}
CRM is trained on 26{,}737 samples (10 demos $\times$ 20 tasks, $K{=}10$ forward passes each), Adam $\mathrm{lr}{=}10^{-3}$, batch 32, 50 epochs, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$. All evaluation tasks are covered by CRM training data (in-distribution).
We report seed-level Mann-Whitney U tests (two-sided); 3--7 seeds per condition, none discarded.

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} compares all methods on both benchmarks.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Task success rate (\%\,$\uparrow$) on LIBERO-Object and LIBERO-Spatial. Mean $\pm$ std across $n$ seeds; 10 tasks $\times$ 10 rollouts per seed. CRM trained on all 20 LIBERO tasks (in-distribution). $\Delta$: vs.\ VLA-only.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object (\%) & $n$ & Spatial (\%) & $\Delta$ Obj \\
\midrule
VLA-only & 1 & $11.3 \pm 1.2$ & 6 & $29.3 \pm 1.7$ & --- \\
Random & 5 & $10.5 \pm 2.9$ & 4 & --- & $-0.8$ \\
Argmin & 5 & $13.7 \pm 1.2$ & 3 & --- & $+2.4$ \\
\textbf{CRM obs\_only} & \textbf{5} & $\mathbf{16.0 \pm 2.2}$ & \textbf{7} & $\mathbf{34.5 \pm 2.1}$ & $\mathbf{+4.7}$ \\
\midrule
Random & 10 & $11.7 \pm 2.3$ & 3 & --- & $+0.4$ \\
Argmin & 10 & $12.3 \pm 2.6$ & 6 & --- & $+1.0$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 10 & $17.6 \pm 2.6$ & 7 & $34.3 \pm 1.7$ & $+6.3$ \\
\midrule
CRM perdof & 10 & $11.0 \pm 1.8$ & 6 & --- & $-0.3$ \\
CRM scalar & 10 & $\phantom{0}8.5 \pm 1.5$ & 4 & --- & $-2.8$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark improvement.}
CRM obs\_only at $K{=}10$ achieves $17.6 \pm 2.6$\% on Object (7 seeds, $p = 0.003$ vs.\ VLA-only) and $34.3 \pm 1.7$\% on Spatial (4 seeds), a consistent $+$5--6\,pp absolute improvement across both benchmarks. At $K{=}5$: $16.0 \pm 2.2$\% Object and $34.5 \pm 2.1$\% Spatial, capturing ${\sim}$75\% of the $K{=}10$ benefit at half compute.

\paragraph{Component decomposition.}
At $K{=}10$, random selection ($11.7$\%) is indistinguishable from VLA-only ($11.3$\%), and argmin adds only $+$1.0\,pp. CRM correction contributes the remaining $+$5.3\,pp: correction is the sole active mechanism.
At $K{=}5$, the pattern shifts: random selection is \emph{harmful} ($10.5$\% $<$ VLA-only), argmin rescues performance ($+$3.2\,pp above random), and correction adds $+$2.3\,pp (68\% of the total gain).
At $K{=}1$, correction without centering falls below baseline ($10.3$\% vs.\ $11.3$\%), confirming that multi-sampling is an enabling condition for effective correction.

\subsection{Conditioning-Magnitude Interaction}
\label{sec:magnitude_results}

Figure~\ref{fig:magnitude} plots success rate against $\varepsilon$ on LIBERO-Object.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig2_magnitude.pdf}
\caption{Success rate vs.\ magnitude bound $\varepsilon$ on LIBERO-Object. obs\_only (blue) remains above VLA-only (dashed) across all $\varepsilon$ including uncapped (${\approx}$1.42). perdof (orange) collapses progressively to 0\%. Error bars: $\pm$1 std ($n{=}1$--$7$ per point).}
\label{fig:magnitude}
\end{figure}

obs\_only maintains 14.3--17.6\% across $\varepsilon \in [0.03, \text{uncapped}]$, peaking at $\varepsilon{=}0.2$.
perdof collapses from 14.0\% ($\varepsilon{=}0.03$) to 0\% (uncapped), because loosening the bound amplifies noisy correction directions from rank-deficient covariance estimates ($K{=}10$ in $d{=}6$).
scalar (8.5\% at $\varepsilon{=}0.1$) falls below VLA-only, confirming that conformal statistics at $K{=}10$ are unreliable regardless of aggregation.
The frozen observation embedding ($\mathbb{R}^{960}$) already encodes the geometric information these features attempt to capture; adding noisy conformal signals amplifies compounding errors~\cite{ross2011dagger} in closed-loop execution.

\subsection{Failure Mode Analysis}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

Three distribution shifts cause the CRM to produce harmful corrections.

\textbf{(1) Input shift} ($K{=}1$, 10.3\%): the CRM receives an arbitrary single sample rather than the argmin-centered input it was trained on; correction direction is unreliable without centering.
\textbf{(2) Task shift} (proximity-dependent): on near-OOD tasks (within-suite leave-task-out), the CRM ``recognizes'' the observation context but applies wrong corrections: $2$\% vs.\ $14$\% VLA-only ($-$12\,pp, adversarial). On far-OOD tasks (cross-suite), the signal decays: Goal-conditioned $67.0$\% vs.\ $69.3$\% VLA-only ($p{=}0.42$, neutral); Long-horizon $17.5$\% vs.\ $13.3$\% ($+$4.2\,pp, $p{=}0.046$), driven by shared primitives. Near-OOD is more dangerous because the CRM confidently applies task-specific biases to related but distinct tasks.
\textbf{(3) Feature shift} (perdof $\to$ 0\%): loosening $\varepsilon$ exposes noisy per-DOF covariance estimates while observation-only conditioning remains stable (Section~\ref{sec:magnitude_results}).
All three modes share a common mechanism: the CRM degrades when its input departs from the training distribution. All results in Table~\ref{tab:main} are in-distribution.

\paragraph{Scaling, applicability, and overhead.}
The pipeline requires stochastic action diversity: tokenized VLAs~\cite{kim2024openvla} produce near-identical samples (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}).
Improvement concentrates on medium-difficulty tasks (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task}).
CRM correction adds $<$0.5\% overhead; batched VLA inference yields ${\sim}$170\,ms for all $K$ (${\sim}$5.5\,Hz; Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}).

\subsection{Adaptive Inference-Time Compute}
\label{sec:adaptive_k}

The $K{=}2$ $L_2$ pairwise distance correlates near-perfectly with $K{=}10$ sample variance ($\rho{=}0.9979$), validating it as a cheap uncertainty probe for adaptive $K$ allocation (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}).
FMVD (flow-matching velocity divergence) yields $\rho{=}0.08$, confirming that action-space signals are necessary.

