\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented a sample-select-correct pipeline for frozen flow-matching VLAs with a lightweight CRM trained on task-specific VLA rollout data.
On in-distribution LIBERO tasks, correction is the active mechanism ($+$5.3\,pp at $K{=}10$, 68\% of the gain at $K{=}5$), with multi-sampling as an enabling condition that provides centering for bounded correction. Both Object ($+$6.3\,pp) and Spatial ($+$5.0\,pp) benchmarks show consistent improvement.
Three distribution shifts (input, task, feature) form a unified failure framework: the CRM degrades when its input departs from the training distribution. Task shift is proximity-dependent: near-OOD produces adversarial degradation ($-$12\,pp) while far-OOD is neutral, paralleling findings from the adversarial robustness literature.
A $K{=}2$ $L_2$ probe ($\rho{=}0.9979$ vs.\ $K{=}10$ variance) enables adaptive compute allocation, applying full correction only when the VLA is uncertain.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
